From 244c679e47133d0b8fe65b9f2c52ba7ea412fccb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 01:38:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: HDR10 sent as Dolby Vision with metadata
 measured from the picture, as an option

videoscreen.hadesdvfromhdr10, off by default: on a display that takes
native Dolby Vision, an HDR10 video without dynamic metadata of its own is
sent as Dolby Vision profile 8.1, with metadata that Hades measures from
the picture as it plays (tools/dvbridge/dvbridge_measured.h). They are not
the studio's: the setting's help says so, the log says so, and the player
information shows the origin of the metadata as "measured by Hades from the
picture". Real Dolby Vision and HDR10+ are never touched: a stream with an
RPU or HDR10+ metadata in any access unit is not taken, and one taken that
shows either later is given back as the HDR10 it is.

The demuxer makes a stream so on its first access unit that can be
measured (a picture, PQ, no Dolby Vision, no HDR10+, a mastering display in
the container or the unit: CDVHdr10Plus::Measurable()), with the profile
8.1 record of the HDR10+ path, and goes on looking at its units. The
decoder gives each picture profile 8.1 metadata whose level 1 comes from
the static metadata (dvbridge_measured_placeholder()), the same as a
picture that is not measured in time keeps. Where the output is not native
the pictures are HDR10, as they always were: nothing is converted.

The pictures are measured on the GPU ahead of their presentation:
CRenderManager::Render() gives the renderer the pictures queued after the
one presented now (copied under the present lock), and the VAAPI renderer
maps each P010 picture once and hands it to the window system
(DVMeasurePicture()). When a picture is presented, the window system
collects what the GPU has finished, without waiting, and makes its level 1,
its level 5 and its scene refresh from it (DVMeasuredMetadata()). A seek
starts the measurement again and keeps the active area; leaving Dolby
Vision output starts it again entirely. If the measurement cannot be set
up, the pictures go out with the metadata of their static metadata, and
the log says so. Every 240 pictures the log has how many were not measured
in time, how many cuts were found, and what was sent last.
